  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Two levels",
      "title": "One system, two zoom levels",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "A food-delivery app. From far away it is a handful of services — Orders, Couriers, Payments — each with its own data, talking through an API gateway and a stream of events.\n\nUp close, each of those boxes is a program of its own, with classes, tables and rules. System design works at both distances, and they have names: [[high-level design]] (HLD) and [[low-level design]] (LLD).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Neither is “more real”. They answer different questions, for different readers, and change at different speeds."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "zoom": "whole system",
        "unit": "services · stores · queues",
        "output": "architecture diagram"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "The map",
      "title": "High-level design: the map",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "HLD decides **what the parts are and how they connect**: which services exist and what each owns, how they talk (a call, or an event), where each kind of data lives, and what happens when traffic grows or a part fails.\n\nIt is the level of every lesson so far. Its output is a diagram like this one, with the reasoning written next to it.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "A good HLD fits on one page. If a box needs a paragraph to explain, that paragraph belongs to the LLD of that box."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ The whole system is the unit of design"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "zoom": "whole system",
        "unit": "services · stores · queues",
        "output": "architecture diagram"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Trade-offs",
      "title": "HLD decisions are trade-offs",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "Placing an order should not wait for a courier to be found, so Orders **publishes an event** and Couriers picks it up when it can (see *Pub/Sub & Events*).\n\nPayments needs transactions, so it gets a relational database. Couriers send their location every few seconds — tens of thousands of writes a second, each overwriting the last — so that store is a fast key–value one. Each choice comes from the numbers and from what the data needs.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Every arrow and every store on an HLD diagram is a decision with an alternative. Be ready to say what you gave up."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Orders → Couriers: an event, not a call",
        "~ Payments: SQL · locations: key–value"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "zoom": "whole system",
        "unit": "services · stores · queues",
        "output": "architecture diagram"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "cdb": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "pdb": {
