    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "LLD decisions",
      "title": "LLD decisions: states, schemas, edge cases",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "An order moves through states, and LLD pins down which moves are allowed: it can be cancelled while `placed` or `paid`, but not once the kitchen has started.\n\nIt also settles the table (columns, types, an index on `(user_id, created_at)` for “my orders”) and the awkward cases: what if the payment call times out, or the same order arrives twice?",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Most production bugs live at this level: a state change nobody allowed for, a missing index, a retry that charges twice."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Order state machine",
        "+ Table and index design",
        "+ Idempotency key on POST /orders"
      ],
